      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a21.19.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g21.19.18', 'g21.19.33'],
          lemma: "φύλλα μόνον … ἐξηράνθη",
          title: "The withered fig tree — the only miracle that destroys",
          body: "This scene bothers readers, and it should be allowed to: it is the **only destructive miracle** in the gospels, and on its surface it looks petulant — hungry man curses tree (Mark adds, disarmingly, ”for it was not the season for figs”). The key is that Matthew has sandwiched it into the temple sequence — city, temple, Bethany, *fig tree*, temple — and the fig tree is Scripture's standing symbol for **Israel inspected for fruit**: Jeremiah 8:13 (*no figs on the fig tree; the leaves are withered*), Micah 7:1, Hosea 9:10. This particular tree advertises: it is in full **leaf**, and on a fig tree leaves normally accompany the early fruit — the foliage is a promise the branches don't keep. Φύλλα μόνον, *leaves only*, is the entire indictment, and it is the temple's: courts thundering with liturgy, sacrifice, expertise — the canopy magnificent, the fruit (justice, mercy, the prayer of the nations) absent. What Jeremiah and the others said in oracles, Jesus performs as a sign: an acted parable of the verdict he has just delivered with the tables, and a preview of the sentence forty years out. Read it whole and the harshness relocates: the miracle is not temper at a plant but grief over a nation, compressed into one roadside emblem — and its lesson, pressed home in the next verse, turns immediately to the disciples' own root system: what withers or moves is decided by πίστις, not foliage.",
        },
      ],
